{"model":"gpt-4.1-nano","max_tokens":1024,"messages":[{"role":"system","content":"You are a helpful assistant."},{"role":"user","content":"I'm preparing a short teaching note for a chat application. Explain in two sentences why an append-only event log differs from a derived context view. Use a concrete chat-history example."},{"role":"assistant","content":"# Append-Only Event Log vs. Derived Context View\n\nAn append-only event log records every message event that occurs (e.g., \"User A sent 'Hello'\" at 2:15 PM, \"User B sent 'Hi there'\" at 2:16 PM), preserving the complete immutable history, whereas a derived context view is a computed snapshot showing only the current state needed for the application (e.g., the last 50 messages with formatting, read status, and reactions applied). The event log serves as the source of truth for reconstruction and auditing, while the context view is optimized for real-time chat display and is regenerated as new events arrive."},{"role":"user","content":"Using your example, give one sentence explaining why rendering that saved log offline should not contact the model again."},{"role":"assistant","content":"Because the event log contains all the raw data needed to deterministically reconstruct the exact chat history that occurred—no model inference is required, only replaying the stored events in order to regenerate the context view."}]}
